{"type":"thinking","text":"Gate 5: REPO RULES triggers for my actions:\n- Add files / write docs: maybe prevent-overengineering? I'm writing research artifacts, not code. The trigger \"Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency\" — writing iteration files is required by workflow, not adding features. Might still load? It says \"add a file\" broadly. Hmm.\n- \"Say done / report result / close out turn / write completion summary\" → evidence-and-proof. Definitely load.\n- \"Hand work to CLI executor / fan-out lineage / deep loop / compose prompt\" → delegation-and-orchestration. I'm executing a fan-out lineage; load.\n- \"Write any substantive reply\" → communication + communication-prose. Load.\n- \"End a turn / report work done\" → communication-handoff. Load.\n- \"Present recommendation / report what a long run found\" → communication-decisions. Load.\n- \"Answer without certainty / hit contradiction\" → uncertainty-and-honesty maybe.\n- \"Delete/overwrite/... change a shared contract\" → blast-radius? I'm not deleting, but the workflow uses gateway writes to ledgers. Probably not needed. But \"overwrite\" maybe not.\n- \"Diagnose failure\" not yet.\n- \"answer the actual request\" maybe not.\n- \"scope discipline\" — I must not touch outside lineage; load perhaps.\n- \"skill-hub-routing\" no.\n\nLoading 6-7 files consumes context. But Gate 6 requires communication.md and communication-prose.md before every substantive reply, plus communication-decisions.md before a recommendation/long stretch, communication-handoff.md before ending a turn, answer-the-actual-request.md before warning/narrowing/declining/pricing. The final answer is just one line, not substantive. But I'll comply enough. The most relevant operational ones: evidence-and-proof, delegation-and-orchestration, scope-discipline, and communication rules. Let me load them. They're each maybe 5-15KB. That's fine.\n\nActually, my context budget is large. Let me read them via read tool. But each read is a \"tool call\" in my session, not the iteration's budget (the 12-call budget applies per iteration for the leaf; I've already used many since I'm also setup). The workflow execution itself isn't bound by the leaf's 12-call budget? The prompt says \"CLI OUTPUT CONTRACT binds every iteration\" and \"Read ... follow their OUTPUT CONTRACT\". The 12 tool-call max is for the leaf iteration. I'm running the whole loop inline; realistically I need more. But the task explicitly says the YAML's per-iteration executor-dispatch steps are satisfied by this process. I should keep the iteration effective but not artificially constrained.\n\nLet me read the rules in one batch. Actually, let me prioritize:\n1. `evidence-and-proof.md` (completion claims)\n2. `delegation-and-orchestration.md` (lineage orchestration)\n3. `scope-discipline.md` (write surface)\n4. `communication.md` + `communication-prose.md` (final answer)\n5. `communication-handoff.md` (final line? minimal)\n6. `uncertainty-and-honesty.md` maybe.\n\nTo save context, I could read only the first three + communication. Gate 6 says load communication/communication-prose before every substantive reply — the final output is one line, maybe not substantive. But let's load communication anyway.\n\nHmm, the instructions in AGENTS.md are extensive and the real task matters. I'll load four files: evidence-and-proof, delegation-and-orchestration, scope-discipline, communication. And maybe communication-prose briefly. Let me do that.\n\nAlso inspect the gateway frame content and lock coordinator state to understand if a run is already open. Let me check quickly.\n\nAnd the `.gitignore` in containment baseline indicates the baseline scan. Fine.\n\nLet me batch reads."}
