{"type":"tool_call","callId":"call_02_quh8Ezw3hGGJIMjEyTZs7527","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"call_00_uXGmbFRl7o4o9TAHkdo67154","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.4\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"call_01_uMqRHHWtg81MeImWDGzk3925","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\n3: description: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"orchestrate posture\"\n6:   - \"one model is one opinion\"\n7:   - \"second lens\"\n8:   - \"diverge the lens\"\n9:   - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n10:   - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n11:   - \"accepting a return\"\n12:   - \"the delegate said complete\"\n13:   - \"verify the citation\"\n14:   - \"scope travels with the work\"\n15:   - \"bind write authority\"\n16:   - \"read the executor contract\"\n17:   - \"delegate or not\"\n18:   - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n19:   - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n20:   - \"two delegates disagree\"\n21:   - \"fan out lineage\"\n22:   - \"sub-agent\"\n23:   - \"CLI executor\"\n24:   - \"deep loop\"\n25:   - \"sub-agent result\"\n26:   - \"delegate to another model\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.0.1.2\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Delegation and orchestration\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all.\n40: - About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.\n41: - About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.\n42: - About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.\n43: - About to answer a judgment question, \"is this the right design\", \"is this rule needed\", \"which approach is better\", from your own reading alone.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:\n48: brief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the\n49: repository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH\n54: \n55: One check comes first: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs\n56: a brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to\n57: write the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).\n58: \n59: The failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.\n60: It has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.\n61: \n62: > **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a\n63: > vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement. What would change\n64: > them: a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as\n65: > well-grounded as a specified one.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH\n70: \n71: Five things, in order. None is optional.\n72: \n73: 1. **Read the executor's own contract**, per `AGENTS.md` §10 Operational Mandates, the CLI dispatch bullet.\n74: 2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.\n75: \n76:    **The freeze runs both ways.** While a fan-out lineage runs, the orchestrator\n77:    changes nothing in the repository outside that lineage's directory until the run\n78:    settles, because the runner's write containment reverts every out-of-lineage change\n79:    and fails the run.\n80: 3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).\n81: 4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,\n82:    a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations.\n83: 5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker obeys\n84:    the same gates you do, including the ones that wait for an operator. Nobody is at its\n85:    prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment variable that\n86:    waives a gate is not enough: a model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer\n87:    in the prompt. Give the decision, not permission to skip it.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE\n92: \n93: A brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back a sample from a distribution, not\n94: a finding about this repository.\n95: \n96: **Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been\n97: ruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the\n98: requirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.\n99: \n100: **Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. Ask the question you actually have.\n101: \n102: The failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent\n103: restatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION\n108: \n109: For a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this\n110: file say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will\n111: check.\n112: \n113: For a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which\n114: of two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:\n115: \n116: - **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against\n117:   the opposite conclusion.\n118: - **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. \"Is this\n119:   rule needed\" becomes \"find the commits where its absence cost us something.\"\n120: - **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.\n121: \n122: Agreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED\n127: \n128: A delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself, see\n129: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7. Four checks before you quote a return:\n130: \n131: - **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the\n132:   cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.\n133: - **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.\n134: - **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run\n135:   describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.\n136: - **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:\n137:   a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that\n138:   wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts\n139:   exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.\n140: \n141: The failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's\n142: confidence attached and none of your verification.\n143: \n144: **When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails\n145: verification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for\n146: what the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could\n147: not answer is evidence about the question.\n148: \n149: **Persist your side too.** Keep the brief, the checks you ran and the verdict, in\n150: whatever artifact owns the work.\n151: \n152: **When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** It means the question was\n153: underspecified or the evidence is thin. Find which, and say which. A tally is not a finding.\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION\n158: \n159: Skipping delegation because you already know the answer is the same single-lens\n160: failure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a\n161: judgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or\n162: say plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.\n163: \n164: The failure this prevents: shipping a con","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"call_02_quh8Ezw3hGGJIMjEyTZs7527","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.\n\n(End of file - total 158 lines)\n</content>"}
